{:edb/db-before-t 1601 :edb/db-after-t 1602 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534914 50 #inst "2026-10-08T10:52:27.138Z" 13194139534914 true] [17592186171821 1100 "Draw a diagram that represents the vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)= -2xy \\, \\mathbf{i} + x^2 \\, \\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n at the points $(-2,1), (-1,-2),$ and $(1,-1).$" 13194139534914 true] [17592186171821 1100 "Draw a diagram that represents the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)= -2xy \\, \\mathbf{i} + x^2 \\, \\mathbf{j}$ at the points $(-2,1), (-1,-2)$, and $(1,-1)$." 13194139534914 false] [17592186171821 1145 "To sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = -2xy \\, \\mathbf{i} + x^2 \\, \\mathbf{j} \\,\n$$\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\mathbf{F} ( - 2 , 1 ) & = - 2 ( - 2 ) ( 1 ) \\, \\mathbf{i} + ( - 2 {)}^{2} \\, \\mathbf{j} = 4 \\, \\mathbf{i} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\mathbf{F} ( - 1 , - 2 ) & = - 2 ( - 1 ) ( - 2 ) \\, \\mathbf{i} + ( - 1 {)}^{2} \\, \\mathbf{j} = - 4 \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\mathbf{F} ( 1 , - 1 ) & = - 2 ( 1 ) ( - 1 ) \\, \\mathbf{i} + {1}^{2} \\, \\mathbf{j} = 2 \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}\\end{array}$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3344/Source/Images/ebb132b3a8cdd98716106f9ef1c3b4a7.png)" 13194139534914 false] [17592186171821 1145 "To sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $\\mathbf F(x,y) = -2xy \\, \\mathbf{i} + x^2 \\, \\mathbf{j} \\,\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-2,1) & =-2(-2)(1)\\,i+(-2)^{2}\\,j=4\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ F(-1,-2) & =-2(-1)(-2)\\,i+(-1)^{2}\\,j=-4\\,i+j \\\\ F(1,-1) & =-2(1)(-1)\\,i+{1}^{2}\\,j=2\\,i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/cf51cba8548d1f342a9dd5c06b533782feb955fca4b90adfac5b6988d7e2e470.png)" 13194139534914 true] [17592186171822 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/2966d95bee0326ed5bf5a5fec9313d15bacd86334f9e9d144ff366bee63d9de3.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?\n\n- $\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}$\n\n- $\\mathbf G(x,y) = 2 \\, \\mathbf{i}$\n\n- $\\mathbf H(x,y) = -x \\, \\mathbf{i}$" 13194139534914 true] [17592186171822 1100 "Which of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3344/Source/Images/a781f1f2da9a61eb9baa82c2c759f6cf.png)\n\n1. $\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}$\n2. $\\mathbf G(x,y) = 2 \\, \\mathbf{i}$\n3. $\\mathbf H(x,y) = -x \\, \\mathbf{i}$" 13194139534914 false] [17592186171822 1145 "We notice the following:\n\n- The vertical component (i.e., the $\\mathbf j$ component) is zero for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' horizontal components (i.e., the $\\mathbf i$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf G(x,y) = 2 \\, \\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{H}(x,y) = -x\\, \\mathbf{i}.$" 13194139534914 true] [17592186171822 1145 "We notice the following:\n\n- The vertical component (i.e., the $\\mathbf j$ component) is zero for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n$\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}$\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' horizontal components (i.e., the $\\mathbf i$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n$\\mathbf G(x,y) = 2 \\, \\mathbf{i}$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{H}(x,y) = -x\\, \\mathbf{i}\n$$" 13194139534914 false] [17592186171826 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/de305db47bcfae58b75797e88ae4a7c7825174bd58d77991ba8dfb4c0b8a13cb.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?\n\n- $\\mathbf  F(x,y,z) = \\mathbf j + \\mathbf k$\n\n- $\\mathbf  G(x,y,z) = \\mathbf k$\n\n- $\\mathbf  H(x,y,z) = z\\,\\mathbf k$\n\n- $\\mathbf  K(x,y,z) = -\\mathbf k$" 13194139534914 true] [17592186171826 1100 "Which of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3344/Source/Images/f7b756645ce2d0592187333f7659de9e.png)\n\n1. $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\mathbf j + \\mathbf k$\n2. $\\mathbf G(x,y,z) = \\mathbf k$\n3. $\\mathbf H(x,y,z) = z\\,\\mathbf k$\n4. $\\mathbf K(x,y,z) = -\\mathbf k$" 13194139534914 false] [17592186171826 1145 "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $z$-axis. So, the $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\mathbf j + \\mathbf k\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $z$-direction, so the $\\mathbf k$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf G(x,y,z) = \\mathbf k\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $\\mathbf i$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf H(x,y,z) = z\\,\\mathbf k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf K(x,y,z) = -\\mathbf k.$" 13194139534914 true] [17592186171826 1145 "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $z$-axis. So, the $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n$\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\mathbf j + \\mathbf k$\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $z$-direction, so the $\\mathbf k$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n$\\mathbf G(x,y,z) = \\mathbf k$\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $\\mathbf i$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n$\\mathbf H(x,y,z) = z\\,\\mathbf k$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf K(x,y,z) = -\\mathbf k\n$$" 13194139534914 false] [17592186172015 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319421 13194139534914 true] [17592186172015 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319428 13194139534914 true] [17592186172015 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319435 13194139534914 true] [17592186172015 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319442 13194139534914 true] [17592186172015 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319449 13194139534914 true] [17592186172016 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319379 13194139534914 true] [17592186172016 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319386 13194139534914 true] [17592186172016 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319393 13194139534914 true] [17592186172016 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319400 13194139534914 true] [17592186172016 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319407 13194139534914 true] [17592186172020 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319414 13194139534914 true] [17592186172020 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319456 13194139534914 true] [17592186172020 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319463 13194139534914 true] [17592186172214 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534914 true] [17592186172214 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/cfc15b6202167211b7d6c0e8f6635dd9e571810d5296a209a4c317350604c4a4.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" 13194139534914 true] [17592186172214 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3344/Source/Images/q-122559.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" 13194139534914 false] [17592186172214 1145 "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $j$ and $k$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y,z)=i+j+k,\\,\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $x$-direction, so the $i$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=-i\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $i$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)={e}^{x}\\,i\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $F(x,y,z)=i.$" 13194139534914 true] [17592186172216 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534914 true] [17592186172216 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/7a0a919af689e9c332ba6ceb3d606208dab9eabadef9b4871ac8e164843ebe88.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" 13194139534914 true] [17592186172216 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3344/Source/Images/q-122664.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" 13194139534914 false] [17592186172216 1145 "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $z$-axis. So, the $i$ and $j$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=i-k\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $z$-direction, so the $k$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y,z)=-k\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $k$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $K(x,y,z)=2\\,k.$" 13194139534914 true] [17592186319379 1017 #edb/ref 17592186319380 13194139534914 true] [17592186319379 1089 "q-122629" 13194139534914 true] [17592186319379 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534914 true] [17592186319379 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/34aca579b2890586365298c764d051f57d4c80977ee975caad505c923343f90d.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" 13194139534914 true] [17592186319379 1113 #uuid "116a3bdd-e190-5b6b-aeb5-2be696056404" 13194139534914 true] [17592186319379 1145 "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $i$ component) is positive for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=-i+x\\,j\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' vertical components (i.e., the $j$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nG(x,y)=i+2\\,j,\\,H(x,y)=2\\,i-j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $K(x,y)=i+x\\,j.$" 13194139534914 true] [17592186319380 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319381 13194139534914 true]